 kodo is an open-source autonomous coding system that turns your Claude Code
  Max subscription into an overnight workforce. You give it a goal — "add
  OAuth login," "refactor the payment module," "migrate from REST to GraphQL"
  — and walk away. kodo's orchestrator breaks the goal into stages, delegates
  work to Claude Code agents, and runs a verification loop where independent
  architect and tester agents review the output before accepting it. You come
  back to committed code and a summary of what happened.

  The quickest way to see what it does is kodo --improve: point it at any
  project and it runs a structured QA audit — static analysis, happy-path
  testing, adversarial/exploratory testing — auto-fixes what it safely can,
  and produces a detailed improvement report. No goal to write, no prompts to
  craft; one command and it works through the codebase on its own. For bigger
  work, you describe a goal in plain English (or a markdown file), optionally
  let kodo's intake interview refine it, and it handles the multi-cycle
  orchestration: thinking, delegating to agents, reading results, resuming
  context when it runs long. The agents themselves are covered by your
  existing Claude Code subscription, so the only real API cost is the
  lightweight orchestrator model coordinating them.